      "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
      "finding": "Within-game defensive scheme change 319/523 (61.0%); cross-game change 298/440 (67.7%); 103/104 units (99.0%) and 52/52 series show a change; transition matrices near-uniform."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-050",
      "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
      "finding": "PUTBACK is top suppressor for 6+ stars (e.g. D. Mitchell 1.178 to 0.884, d=-0.294, n_after=95; B. Beal d=-0.345 n=32; C. Holmgren d=-0.222 n=52). Other tops: TRANSITION (A. Davis -0.058), POST_UP (N. Jokic -0.075), PULL_UP."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF07",
      "status_as_recorded": "supported",
      "finding": "[APPA-049] A2 aftershock 8/8 PTs replicate all-games and playoffs (CUT +0.119 n=21,398 / PO +0.131 n=1,145 through TRANSITION +0.135 n=26,262 / +0.126 n=1,529). Top pair premiums: PUTBACK->TRANSITION +0.084 n=565; CUT->PULL_UP +0.063 n=2,512."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-078",
      "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
      "finding": "90s vs MADE_DEAD: TURNOVER_LIVE dOppPts=+0.510 dNetPts=-0.306 (dOppPPP=+0.055, dOppPoss=+0.298); MISSED_DEAD dNetPts=-0.112; PUTBACK dNetPts=+0.078. Volume (dOppPoss), not PPP, carries most of the live-TO cost."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-005",
      "what_failed": "Attempting to measure defensive scheme changes using inferred labels",
      "why": "A_unanswerable (question not answerable with the data or design); inferred label cannot separate real defensive adjustment from offense executing differently; near-uniform transition matrices consistent with label noise."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-050",
      "what_failed": "Measuring superstar sequential suppression without multiplicity correction and with small sample sizes for specific effects",
      "why": "E_unclear (mechanism unclear from the records); No multiplicity correction; n_after is 32-95 for several PUTBACK cells vs thousands of baseline possessions."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-078",
      "what_failed": "Analyzing possession ending state vs next 90-180s volume/points with descriptive statistics only",
      "why": "E_unclear (mechanism unclear from the records); No inferential tests in the core table; live TO raising opponent possession count is largely definitional (steal starts a break)."
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "Sequential effects between possessions exist in basketball, where a preceding play can influence the outcome of a subsequent possession (FAM:SF07, FAM:SF-APPA-050).",
    "There is evidence of a 'defensive aftershock' where, after the opponent runs a certain play type, the next offensive play scores above its baseline (FAM:SF07). This suggests that defensive vulnerability can be a consequence of prior offensive action.",
    "Specific opponent play types can suppress a star player's next-possession PPP (FAM:SF-APPA-050)."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "The specific impact of 'our' team's offensive play X on the 'opponent's next-possession PPP' in a way that directly drives 'AVOID labels'. While sequential effects are shown, the direction (our offense -> opponent offense) and magnitude for specific 'AVOID' thresholds are not established.",
    "A clear definition or methodology for deriving 'AVOID labels' from PPP values.",
    "Whether 'own-sequence lags' (effects of prior plays within the same team's sequence) similarly matter in the context of driving AVOID labels."
